WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:05.047
The deep blues of water, anamorphic&nbsp;
lenses, and a contemplative MCU&nbsp;story. &nbsp;

00:00:05.047 --> 00:00:08.820
Wakanda Forever passionately&nbsp;
honors the legacy of Chadwick Boseman.

00:00:08.820 --> 00:00:12.180
It's no secret that Black Panther:&nbsp;
Wakanda Forever is one of the most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.180 --> 00:00:16.860
highly-anticipated MCU films so far.&nbsp;
The movie serves both as a sequel to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.860 --> 00:00:22.680
the beloved 2018 blockbuster Black Panther and&nbsp;
as the climactic finale to Marvel's Phase 4.

00:00:22.680 --> 00:00:27.815
And​​ yet, overshadowing all of the excitement&nbsp;
for Wakanda Forever is the notable absence of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.815 --> 00:00:32.760
actor Chadwick Boseman, who tragically passed&nbsp;
away in 2020 following an extended battle with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.760 --> 00:00:36.960
colon cancer. From what we've seen thus&nbsp;
far in the trailers for Wakanda Forever,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:41.280
it's clear that Boseman's legacy will&nbsp;
be a major component of the film itself.

00:00:41.280 --> 00:00:45.540
Indeed, it appears that much of the film's&nbsp;
story will be informed by the devastating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.540 --> 00:00:49.800
loss of Chadwick Boseman. Director Ryan&nbsp;
Coogler confirmed earlier this year when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.800 --> 00:00:53.340
he said that the cast and crew put their&nbsp;
"love for Chadwick" into the film while&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.340 --> 00:00:58.560
presenting a preview for the movie at San Diego&nbsp;
Comic-Con. In fact, Boseman's impact on Wakanda&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.560 --> 00:01:03.720
Forever is so great that even the film's&nbsp;
visual style was influenced by his passing.

00:01:03.720 --> 00:01:07.980
In a recent interview with Entertainment Weekly,&nbsp;
Coogler mentioned how the visual style of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.980 --> 00:01:12.480
movie is supposed to reflect the immense&nbsp;
loss felt after Boseman's death. He said:

00:01:12.480 --> 00:01:17.760
"I think this film has the fog of loss over&nbsp;
it. Anamorphic lenses warp the image a little&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.760 --> 00:01:22.260
bit. Sometimes when you go through profound&nbsp;
loss, it can warp how you look at the world...&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.260 --> 00:01:27.120
what we were after was just making it feel&nbsp;
tactile, even though it felt like a dream."

00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:32.220
Coogler went on to explain that this is meant to&nbsp;
craft a film that is both intimate and emotionally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.220 --> 00:01:37.200
poignant while operating on the scale of a&nbsp;
massive superhero blockbuster. In addition to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:42.300
the use of warped lenses, Coogler addressed how&nbsp;
the shift in visual style includes an emphasis&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.300 --> 00:01:46.860
on water and the color blue — something that&nbsp;
should come as no surprise since the film's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.860 --> 00:01:52.140
primary antagonist, Namor the Sub-Mariner,&nbsp;
is the ruler of a vast undersea kingdom.

00:01:52.140 --> 00:01:54.565
"Wakanda will fall."

00:01:54.565 --> 00:01:58.560
Of course, since loss and grief have so&nbsp;
heavily informed the storytelling and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.560 --> 00:02:04.080
style of Wakanda Forever, it's no surprise that&nbsp;
the experience of making it was melancholic for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.080 --> 00:02:08.040
those who knew and worked with Boseman on the&nbsp;
previous movie. While speaking to Collider,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.040 --> 00:02:12.540
Winston Duke, who plays M'Baku, explained that&nbsp;
shooting the sequel was strange without their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.540 --> 00:02:15.720
old colleague on the set. However,&nbsp;
the experience helped them come to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.720 --> 00:02:19.320
terms with his death and understand&nbsp;
the grieving process. Duke said:

00:02:19.320 --> 00:02:23.100
"It was something that we dealt with, every&nbsp;
day, and something that we got to understand,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.100 --> 00:02:27.780
every day, that we're not the only people&nbsp;
grieving. Not only the people who are in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.780 --> 00:02:30.000
front of camera are grieving, it's everybody."

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:34.440
Elsewhere, Lupita Nyong'o, who portrays&nbsp;
Nakia, recalled having to meditate, pray,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.440 --> 00:02:38.400
and reflect in order to come to grips with&nbsp;
the new movie existing without her friend&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.400 --> 00:02:42.840
and co-star. It took the actress a while to&nbsp;
adjust to the idea of a sequel without Boseman,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.840 --> 00:02:47.012
but she understood that changes were&nbsp;
necessary. She told The New York Times:

00:02:47.012 --> 00:02:50.580
"I found myself having to radically&nbsp;
accept that this was going to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.580 --> 00:02:54.180
different and that showing up with as&nbsp;
much openness as possible was key."

00:02:54.180 --> 00:02:59.040
Wakanda Forever was certainly an emotional&nbsp;
experience for everyone involved — but the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.040 --> 00:03:03.240
movie promises to honor the legacy&nbsp;
of both Boseman and King T'Challa.

